Subject: Get objective function derivative within cpp code
Message-ID: <oejkgLVoSag.caL1jnF4BgAJ@tmb-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/tmb-users/c/oejkgLVoSag/m/caL1jnF4BgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Is there a equivalent of Stan's adj() operator (see page 8 of https://arxiv.org/pdf/1509.07164.pdf) to calculate derivatives of the objective function within the TMB cpp function definition?  Here is some example Stan code (taken from: http://tobiasmadsen.com/2017/03/31/automatic_differentiation_in_r/), see the bit where the gradient is computed:

#include <Rcpp.h>
#include <stan/math.hpp>

using namespace Rcpp;

// [[Rcpp::export]]
NumericVector normLogLikAD(double y_, double mu_, double sigma_) {
  NumericVector ret(4);

  stan::math::var y = y_;
  stan::math::var mu = mu_;
  stan::math::var sigma = sigma_;
  stan::math::var lp = 0;

  lp += -0.5* ((y-mu)/sigma) * ((y-mu)/sigma) - log(sigma) - 0.5 * log(2*PI);

  // Compute Function Value
  ret[0] = lp.val();

  // Compute Gradient
  lp.grad();
  ret[1] = y.adj();
  ret[2] = mu.adj();
  ret[3] = sigma.adj();

  // Memory is allocated on a global stack
  stan::math::recover_memory();
  stan::math::ChainableStack::memalloc_.free_all();

  return ret;
}

